@startuml design_decisions
title Code Executor Sandbox - Design Decisions & Trade-offs
skinparam defaultTextAlignment left
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFFCC
skinparam legendBackgroundColor #F5F5F5

legend top
  |= Decision |= Choice |= Alternatives Considered |
  | Isolation | Docker containers | gVisor, Firecracker, nsjail, chroot |
  | Docker API | CLI (subprocess) | Docker SDK (docker-py) |
  | Security | Defense in Depth | Single layer |
  | Storage | In-memory | Redis, PostgreSQL |
  | Streaming | SSE | WebSocket, Long-polling |
  | Module control | Whitelist | Blacklist |
endlegend

' ==================== DOCKER CLI vs SDK ====================
package "Decision 1: Docker CLI vs SDK" as d1 #LightBlue {
    note as n1
      **Chosen: Docker CLI (subprocess)**

      **Pros:**
      + No additional dependencies
      + Easier to debug (same commands as terminal)
      + Better compatibility across Docker versions
      + Simpler deployment

      **Cons:**
      - Slightly higher latency (~5ms)
      - Need to parse CLI output

      **Why not Docker SDK?**
      * Adds docker-py dependency
      * Version compatibility issues
      * Overkill for simple run/kill operations
      * CLI is more transparent for security audits
    end note
}

' ==================== DEFENSE IN DEPTH ====================
package "Decision 2: Defense in Depth (Two Layers)" as d2 #LightGreen {
    note as n2
      **Chosen: Docker + Python Sandbox**

      **Layer 1: Docker Container**
      * OS-level isolation (namespaces, cgroups)
      * Network isolation (--network=none)
      * Filesystem isolation (--read-only)
      * Resource limits (memory, CPU, PIDs)

      **Layer 2: Python Sandbox**
      * Restricted builtins (no eval, exec, open)
      * Module whitelist (only safe modules)
      * Attribute blocking (__class__, __bases__)

      **Why two layers?**
      * Defense in depth principle
      * If Docker escapes (CVE), Python layer still protects
      * If Python sandbox bypassed, Docker contains damage
      * Each layer catches different attack vectors
    end note
}

' ==================== STORAGE ====================
package "Decision 3: In-Memory Storage" as d3 #LightYellow {
    note as n3
      **Chosen: MemoryStorage (dict)**

      **Pros:**
      + Zero latency
      + No external dependencies
      + Simple to implement
      + Sufficient for interview scope

      **Trade-off:**
      - Lost on restart
      - Single server only

      **Extensibility:**
      TaskStorage is an abstract interface.
      Can easily swap to:
      * RedisStorage (for distributed)
      * PostgresStorage (for persistence)
    end note
}

' ==================== MODULE WHITELIST ====================
package "Decision 4: Module Whitelist vs Blacklist" as d4 #LightCoral {
    note as n4
      **Chosen: Whitelist**

      **Whitelist approach:**
      * Explicitly allow: math, json, datetime, etc.
      * Everything else is denied by default

      **Why not blacklist?**
      * Blacklist: block os, sys, subprocess...
      * Problem: Cannot anticipate all dangerous modules
      * New Python versions add new modules
      * Risk of missing something

      **Security principle:**
      "Deny by default, allow explicitly"

      **Current whitelist (22 modules):**
      math, cmath, decimal, fractions, random,
      statistics, itertools, functools, operator,
      string, textwrap, datetime, calendar, time,
      collections, heapq, bisect, array, copy,
      pprint, enum, dataclasses, re, json
    end note
}

' ==================== CONCURRENCY ====================
package "Decision 5: Concurrency Control" as d5 #Lavender {
    note as n5
      **Chosen: asyncio.Semaphore**

      **Why Semaphore?**
      * Native Python async primitive
      * Efficient queuing (tasks wait, not rejected)
      * No external dependencies
      * Configurable limit

      **Behavior:**
      * Default: 10 concurrent tasks
      * Task 11+ waits in queue
      * Fair ordering (FIFO)

      **Rate Limiting: Token Bucket**
      * 60 requests/minute per IP
      * Burst allowance: 10 requests
      * Gradual refill
    end note
}

' ==================== STREAMING ====================
package "Decision 6: SSE vs WebSocket" as d6 #PaleGreen {
    note as n6
      **Chosen: Server-Sent Events (SSE)**

      **Pros:**
      + HTTP-based (works with proxies, CDNs)
      + Auto-reconnection built-in
      + Simpler than WebSocket
      + One-way is sufficient for output streaming

      **Cons:**
      - One-way only (server to client)
      - No binary data support

      **Why not WebSocket?**
      * Overkill for one-way streaming
      * Requires more complex client code
      * Proxy/firewall issues more common
    end note
}

@enduml
